# Jarvis skill builder

Jarvis — the local assistant that manages this machine's skills — can write,
test and install a new skill from a plain-English request. This meta-skill
hands your request to that pipeline instead of improvising a script here.

## When to use

Use it when the user asks for a capability none of the available skills
provide: converting files, counting or extracting things, bulk renames, and
similar scriptable jobs on local files. Do not use it when an existing skill
already does the job.

## How

Run the builder with the exec tool, passing the user's request verbatim as one
quoted argument:

    node "%BUILDER%" "count how many files of each extension are in ~/Downloads"

Expect it to take a few minutes: a code model is loaded, the script is written,
and its authored tests run in a sandbox that denies network access. The command
prints what happened either way.

## After it succeeds

The output names the new skill and its parameters, and a wrapper for it is
placed in this workspace's skills directory — from the next session it appears
as its own skill. In this session, run it through the shim command the builder
prints.

## Rules

- Pass the user's own words through unchanged; do not rephrase the request.
- If the build is rejected or the model is unreachable, report the printed
  reason and stop; do not write the script yourself instead — a skill from
  this pipeline has been tested and installed under integrity checks, and an
  improvised one has not.
- Never edit anything under Jarvis's own skills directory by hand.
